WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.100
- Welcome everyone.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.320
I'm Priya Natarajan,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.140
I'm the current director

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.780
of the Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities at Yale.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.520
And I am delighted to have you all

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.751
attend today's, "Distinguished
Speaker Series Talk"

00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:23.640
with Kirk Wallace Johnson.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.560
I'm delighted to see you,
although we are still virtual,

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.313
we were are hoping to meet you in person.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.870
First, I would like to
take a moment of silence

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.780
to honor those who are vulnerable

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.930
in the face of the ongoing senseless war.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.300
Our collective humanity is under threat,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.250
and we stand in solidarity

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.900
with all of those who
are suffering right now.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.020
So let's do just take a
few moments of silence

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.523
before we start our
celebratory events today.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.170
Hey, so let's join in to celebrate

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.170
of the wonderful imagination
of a human mind, Kirk's,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.750
and his book, "The Feather
Thief: Beauty, Obsession,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.240
and the Natural History
Heist of the Century."

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.670
But before we set off,

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.860
I would like to thank Mr and Mrs. Richard

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.920
and Barbara Frankie for
their generous support

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.130
of multiple interdisciplinary
activities at Yale.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.460
A flagship of which is the Frankie Program

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.460
in Science and the Humanities.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.650
Thank you so much.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.670
They are in attendance
today with Jane Frankie.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.600
And I know that we have a draw
of a very wide audience today

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.210
from near and far,

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.690
and we are pleased to welcome

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.220
all the enthusiastic
naturalists and bird watchers.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.240
In addition to our loyal following

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.800
who have helped us build
and sustain our community

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.495
through the very isolating

00:02:12.495 --> 00:02:16.110
and difficult past two
years of the pandemic.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.700
So glad to be emerging from that darkness

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:22.070
into health, happiness,
and light for all of us.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.020
I just want to remind you that
we are recording this event

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.610
and that all participants will
therefore have their videos

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.570
muted for the duration of the talk.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.030
Please feel free to type in your questions

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.300
and you may submit them at
any time in the Q&amp;A feature

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.223
in our webinar.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.900
Well, I want to start today

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.440
by introducing someone who
doesn't need an introduction.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.360
Rick Prum,

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.390
the William Robertson Coe
Professor of ornithology,

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.500
and the curator of ornithology,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.590
and head curator of vertebrae zoology

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.710
at Yale's Peabody Museum
of Natural History.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.238
Rick is the former director
of the Frankie Program

00:03:00.238 --> 00:03:04.170
and is a renowned scientist
and a claimed author.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.337
Many of you might have
read his best selling,

00:03:07.337 --> 00:03:09.200
"The Evolution of Beauty."

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.730
And I gather, he is a brand new book

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.830
that is expected to be out very soon.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.890
His list of seminal contributions

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.050
to our understanding of
the area of evolution

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.570
and his recognitions and honors,

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.780
awards are too numerous to recount.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.890
So in the interest of time,

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.770
I will hand over the proceedings to him

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.670
in just a little bit this afternoon.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:32.840
But before I do that,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.360
I would like to mention a
couple of our ongoing projects

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.270
in a few upcoming events.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.790
I wanted to you about our
learning by doing project,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.950
which showcases stem videos.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.040
So please contribute, take
a look at our webpage,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.850
and we have a couple of
interesting upcoming events.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:54.880
We have the ongoing inference
project, talk and discussion.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.110
The next talk will be
given by Kyle Cranmer

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.270
on Wednesday, March at 4:00 pm on zoom.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:02.103
And the discussion

00:04:02.103 --> 00:04:05.720
will be a week later on
March 16th at the same time,

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.017
with Zenna Tavares.

00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:10.300
"The Distinguished Speaker Series Talk,"

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.620
the next one will be by Carl Zimmer,

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.935
and he will be talking to us on his book,

00:04:14.935 --> 00:04:18.060
"Life's Edge: The Search for
What It Means to Be Alive"

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.630
on Wednesday, March 30th at 4:00 pm.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.480
Both these events will
actually be virtual.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:30.350
We are really hoping to meet
in convenient person in April,

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.130
and we are hoping to debut
that with mapping as knowing

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:38.310
lecture talk by the
musicologist, Victor Coelho,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.890
on April 13th.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:42.240
And we are hoping it will be in person

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.990
in the new humanities squad Durango,

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:46.750
and also watch the space,

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:50.660
the acclaimed jazz musician, Vijay Iyer.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:55.660
We are hoping to have a live
event in April, hopefully.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.460
Anyway, thank you Rick,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.883
for offering to introduce
our wonderful speaker, Kirk.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.350
Take it away.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.183
- Thank you.

00:05:04.183 --> 00:05:05.130
Thanks very much Priya.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:09.760
And thank you for that
overly kind introduction.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.875
It is a pleasure to introduce
Kirk Wallace Johnson,

00:05:12.875 --> 00:05:15.520
bestselling author and journalist.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.520
And if I can add, adventurer
and storyteller par excellence.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.720
Kirk was educated at the
University of Chicago

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.700
and later did a Fulbright scholar research

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.103
in Egypt studying political Islamism.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.860
He worked a afterwards in
Iraq with the U.S. agency

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.650
for international development in Baghdad.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:42.750
And his experience in Iraq,
and following that experience

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.750
was the core of his first book in 2014,

00:05:48.287 --> 00:05:50.060
"To Be a Friend Is Fatal:

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.643
The Fight to Save the
Iraqis America Left Behind"

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.300
This book was described by George Packer

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:06.300
as riveting, darkly
funny, heroic and shaming,

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:11.423
and was a deeply affecting book.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:15.437
His 2019 book,

00:06:15.437 --> 00:06:17.840
"The Feathery Thief: Beauty, Obsession

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.990
and the Natural History
Heist of the Century,"

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:23.125
the subject of today's
lecture, was also bestselling,

00:06:23.125 --> 00:06:26.080
and usually an unusual book

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.120
in the niche of wonder logical true crime.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.743
And that's what we'll
be hearing about today.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:36.577
Kirk has a book coming out in late summer,

00:06:36.577 --> 00:06:39.040
"The Fishermen and the Dragon:

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.940
Fear, Greed, and a Fight for
Justice on the Gulf Coast"

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:46.590
which is about immigrant
Vietnamese fishing communities

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.240
in Texas and Alabama.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.490
So, exciting events to come.

00:06:53.327 --> 00:06:55.137
Kirk's writing has appeared
in "The New Yorker,"

00:06:55.137 --> 00:06:55.987
"The New York Times,"

00:06:55.987 --> 00:06:57.967
"The Washington Post,"

00:06:57.967 --> 00:06:58.937
"The Wall Street Journal,"

00:06:58.937 --> 00:07:01.890
"Foreign Policy," among many others.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.910
His work has appeared
several times as well

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.370
on the NPR show, "This American Life,"

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.403
including two rare hourlong
programs on these things.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.000
Kirk is currently

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.130
a fellow at the USC Annenberg Center

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.530
on Communication Leadership and Policy.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:28.530
Now, in about 2012 or '13 at
the Whitney Humanities Center,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.897
I was sitting down to lunch
when someone asked me,

00:07:32.897 --> 00:07:36.260
"Do you know, or have
you heard of Edwin Rist?"

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:39.110
I almost fell out of my chair

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:42.560
because I thought that I
was maybe alone in the world

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.683
to being obsessed with Edwin Rist.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.930
My questioner told me
that she had a friend

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:52.537
who was also obsessed with Edwin Rist,

00:07:52.537 --> 00:07:54.970
and was thinking of writing a book.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.780
I soon got in touch with Kirk about that

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.950
and began a marvelous correspondence.

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.020
Soon we realized we had to meet

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:09.280
and Kirk visited in New Haven
for a marvelous couple days

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.160
where we talked about feathers endlessly.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.770
And I talked about feathered
anatomy and development

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.113
and on and on.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.200
Really Kirk had sort of
a mini graduate seminar

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.993
in feather biology over those days.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.247
Of course, Kirk went on
to do further research,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:37.730
which is the subject of the book.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.090
So I won't go any further

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:43.420
except to pick one
little bone of contention

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:45.160
that I'm gonna raise.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:48.863
And that is when the book came out,

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.110
I was portrayed

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:55.310
as a one dimensionally
potty mouth Ornithologist

00:08:56.350 --> 00:09:00.320
In fact, that feature came the
teaser at the top of the hour

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.300
in the beginning of act two
in "This American Life."

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:08.227
So much that when my
mother's friends said,

00:09:08.227 --> 00:09:10.810
"I heard your son on NPR."

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.037
And she asked me,

00:09:12.037 --> 00:09:14.465
"Why didn't you tell me
you were gonna be on NPR?"

00:09:14.465 --> 00:09:17.530
I was afraid to tell her.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:22.530
But in fact, I must say in
the years with heavy rotation

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:26.230
of this program in the year
since, every once in a while,

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:28.850
I'll get an email from an
old high school friend,

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.047
or from an acquaintance
from long pal saying,

00:09:31.047 --> 00:09:32.560
"Hey, I heard you on NPR."

00:09:33.676 --> 00:09:36.020
You really always referring
to the same moments.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.830
Now, well, the moment is an enthusiastic

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:43.050
and highly energetic expletive,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.850
which featured in both the book

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:47.700
and in this point in live.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.230
And I just wanna say now,

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:52.310
I had hoped for more balanced portrayal,

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:54.913
but I know in interest of the story

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.380
which was indeed riveting,
that's the source of it.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.933
So I just wanted to report on that.

00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:05.260
And I just want also to tell you

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.320
that Kirk, my mom is
in the audience today.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.170
- Okay.

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:10.142
- And so I'm not-

00:10:10.142 --> 00:10:12.142
- Watch your mouth then-

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:14.123
- Take it away, Kirk,

00:10:14.123 --> 00:10:16.798
and thanks very much for taking the time

00:10:16.798 --> 00:10:19.233
to join us at the Frankie Program.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:24.360
- Thank you for that kind intro, Rick,

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.760
and you have my apologies for that.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.400
I would just say, watch your mouth.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.823
When you talk to a
journalist with the recorder.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:38.490
I wanna thank Yale and
the Frankie for having me.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:43.490
This is, I think, part of the appeal

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:47.900
that people have
expressed about this book,

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.140
is that it there's a bit of escapism to it

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.760
and we're in some
particularly heavy times.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:56.260
And so even having written it,

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:00.670
I still enjoy the opportunity
to kind of dive back

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:01.623
into this story.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.970
Since we have the advantage

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:10.800
to look at some pictures
and videos and audio,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:12.923
I'm gonna share my screen here.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:19.260
And I'm gonna open with a kind
of startling piece of audio

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.400
that happened during
one of the interviews.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.320
Don't worry, Rick, it's not yours,

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.233
but this is probably two
years into my investigation.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:43.000
Speaking to a South African
Victorian salmon fly tire,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:46.920
who I suspected as having
some of the stolen birds

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:48.070
that you'll hear about.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:51.713
- [Man] The fact that it happened,

00:11:51.713 --> 00:11:53.513
I don't think is shocking all,

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:57.430
and it's not because of the
feverish demand or anything.

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.350
It's where something is case,

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:03.363
where something is really
difficult to get old of.

00:12:03.363 --> 00:12:06.160
And there's enough people
that want to forget

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.793
about the feverish demand around it.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:12.140
People are creative,

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:15.197
they're gonna find ways
to source what they need.

00:12:15.197 --> 00:12:18.040
And really is as simple as that.

00:12:20.917 --> 00:12:25.010
In terms of severity
of the crime and plea,

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.370
I dunno if you can quote on
anything, I don't really care,

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.570
but in terms of severity of crimes,

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:32.910
this, in my opinion,

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.600
just doesn't rate that ideally.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:38.563
These are skins that are
sitting in cupboard somewhere.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.280
Do I condone it?

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:42.400
No, not at all.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.000
Anything that is wrong is wrong.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:45.370
Wrong is wrong, and right is right.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:46.780
It's as simple as that.

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.877
For me, the biggest shock
was that it was the Edwin.

00:12:51.431 --> 00:12:54.281
- Okay, now I'm gonna do a
quick reading from the prologue.

00:12:54.281 --> 00:12:58.990
I don't really like
reading very, very often,

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:03.620
partly because I have a
five-year-old and a four-year-old,

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.540
and I have this habit of kind of breaking

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.350
into a sing song, Dr. Sue's voice.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:10.340
So forgive me if that happens,

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:14.180
but I'm gonna read you a brief
passage from the prologue,

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.980
just that kind of set the
scene for those of you

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:17.393
who haven't read the book.

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:23.290
This pertains to the
events of June 23rd, 2009.

00:13:25.677 --> 00:13:27.920
"By the time Edwin Rist
stepped off the train

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.810
on to the platform at Tring,
40 miles north of London,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.290
it was already quite late.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.740
The residents of the sleepy
town had finished their suppers.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.380
The little ones were in bed.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.080
As he began a long walk into town,

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.490
the train glided off into darkness.

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.560
A few hours earlier Edwin had performed

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:45.260
in the Royal Academy of
Music's, London Soundscapes,

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:48.560
a celebration of Haydn,
Handel, and Mendelssohn.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.130
Before the concert, he'd
packed a pair of latex gloves,

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.427
a miniature LED flashlight, a wire cutter,

00:13:53.427 --> 00:13:55.090
and a diamond-blade glass cutter

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.990
in a large rolling suitcase,

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:59.870
and stowed it in his concert hall locker.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:03.310
He bore a passing resemblance
to a lanky Pete Townshend.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:05.930
Intense eyes, prominent
nose, and a mop of hair,

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:08.020
although instead of shredding a Fender,

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:09.363
Edwin played the flute.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.340
There was already a new moon that evening,

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:15.160
making the gloomy stretch
of road even darker.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.120
For nearly an hour, he
dragged his suitcase

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:18.980
through the mud and
gravel skirting the road,

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:21.890
under gnarly old trees strangled with ivy.

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:24.500
A car blasted by, its headlights blinding.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:27.810
Adrenaline coursing, he
knew he was getting close.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.690
The entrance to the market town of Tring

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:33.430
is guarded by a 16th century
pub called the Robin Hood.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:36.710
A few roads beyond, nestled
between the old Tring Brewery

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:41.710
and an HSBC branch, lies the
entrance to Public Footpath 37.

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.330
Known to locals as Bank Alley,

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:45.310
the footpath isn't more
than eight feet wide

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:48.770
and is framed by
seven-foot-high brick walls.

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:51.450
Edwin slipped into the
alley into total darkness.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:52.540
He groped his way along

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.650
until he was standing
directly behind the building,

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:56.653
he'd spent months casing.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:00.140
All that separated him
from it was the wall.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:02.350
Capped with three rusted
strands of barbed wire,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.600
it might have thwarted his plans

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.110
where it not for the wire cutter.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.170
After clearing an opening,

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:08.990
he lifted the suitcase to the
ledge, hoisted himself up,

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:10.600
and glanced anxiously about.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:11.803
No sign of the guard.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.410
He reached toward the
window with the glass cutter

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:17.810
and began to grind it along the pane.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:20.420
Cutting glass was harder than
he had anticipated, though,

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:21.960
and as he struggled to carve an opening,

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:23.800
the glass cutter slipped from his hand.

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.160
His mind raced, was this a sign?

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.460
He was thinking about bailing
out of the whole crazy scheme

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:29.293
when that voice,

00:15:29.293 --> 00:15:31.720
the one that had urged him
onward these past months,

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.140
shouted, wait a minute!

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:36.960
You can't give up now.
You've come all this way!

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.900
He crawled back down and picked up a rock.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:41.350
Steadying himself atop the wall,

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:43.040
he peered around in search of guards

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:44.970
before bashing the window out,

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:47.670
wedging his suitcase through
the shard-strewn opening,

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:50.483
and climbing into the British
Natural History Museum.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.330
Unaware that he had just tripped an alarm

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.120
in a security guard's office,

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:57.710
Edwin pulled out the LED light,

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:59.350
which cast a faint glow in front of him

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.680
as he made his way down the
hallways toward the vault,

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.900
just as he'd rehearsed in his mind.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.530
He wheeled his suitcase quietly

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:06.960
through corridor after corridor,

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.060
drawing ever closer to
the most beautiful things

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.300
he had ever seen.

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:11.410
If he pulled this off,

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:14.020
they would bring him
fame, wealth and prestige.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:17.390
They would solve his
problems. He deserved them.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:18.360
He entered the vault,

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:20.670
its 100s of large white steel cabinets

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.730
standing in rows like
sentries, and got to work.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:24.950
He pulled out the first drawer,

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:26.860
catching a waft of mothballs.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.380
Quivering beneath his fingertips

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.040
were a dozen red-ruffed fruitcrows,

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:31.730
gathered by naturalists and biologists

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:34.380
over 100s of years from
the forests and jungles

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:35.403
of South America.

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.110
Their coppery orange feathers glimmered

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:39.680
despite the faint light.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.640
Each bird, maybe a foot and
a half from beak to tail,

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.490
lay on its back in funerary repose,

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:47.560
eye sockets filled with cotton,

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:50.070
feet folded close against the body.

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:51.330
He unzipped the suitcase

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:52.890
and began to fill it with the birds,

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.057
emptying one drawer after another."

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.350
Now I'm gonna interrupt myself.

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:03.700
I'm under no obligation
to say this or relay this,

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:07.050
but I'm feeling generous towards
the British Museum today.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.713
So I will say that this, of
the everything in the book,

00:17:09.713 --> 00:17:14.070
this is the one thing that they dispute.

00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:15.880
But, "Down in the security office,

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:19.060
the guard was fixated on
a small television screen.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:20.650
Engrossed in a soccer match,

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:22.600
he hadn't yet noticed the alarm indicator

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:24.660
blinking on a nearby panel."

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.910
This led to a very surreal
exchange with the British Museum,

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:30.330
where they said there
wasn't a soccer match

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.000
happening that night.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.883
I had to prove that there was,

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:37.730
and then they said that the
guard doesn't like soccer,

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:41.760
to which I replied, he's
British, I'm sure he does.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.900
But anyways, moving on.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:45.600
Edwin opened the next cabinet

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.160
to reveal dozens of
resplendent quetzal skins

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:51.150
gathered in the 1880s from
the Chiriqui cloud forests

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:52.770
of western Panama,

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:55.830
a species now threatened
by widespread deforestation

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:58.170
and protected by international treaties.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:00.190
At nearly four feet in length,

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.390
the birds were particularly
difficult to stuff

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:03.710
into his suitcase,

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:06.010
but he maneuvered 39 of them inside

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:09.603
by gently curling their
sweeping tails into tight coils.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:15.970
Edwin had lost track of how
long he'd been in the vault,

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:17.720
when he finally wheeled his suitcase

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.490
to a stop before a large cabinet.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.457
A small plaque indicated its
contents, 'Paradisaeidae.'

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.923
37 king birds-of-paradise,
swiped in seconds.

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:33.660
24 magnificent riflebirds,
12 superb birds-of-paradise."

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:35.960
And to show you what
we're talking about here.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:44.100
Okay, wrapping up the prologue here,

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:45.463
and then we'll get into it.

00:18:46.427 --> 00:18:49.490
"The guard glanced at the CCTV feed,

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:53.040
an array of shots of the parking
lot and the museum campus.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:56.060
He began his round, pacing the
hallways, checking the doors,

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:58.020
scanning for anything awry.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.910
Edwin had long since lost
count of the number of birds

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:02.630
that passed through his hands.

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:03.490
He had originally planned

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:05.230
to choose only the best of each species,

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.610
but in the excitement of the plunder,

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:07.610
he grabbed and stuffed

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:09.653
until his suitcase could hold no more.

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:13.260
The guard stepped outside
to begin a perimeter check,

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:15.730
glancing up at the windows
and beaming his flashlight

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:19.040
on to the section abutting
the brick wall of Bank Alley.

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:21.090
Edwin stood before the broken window,

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:23.060
now framed with shards of glass.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:25.870
So far everything had
gone according to plan.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:28.360
All that remained was to
climb back out of the window

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:30.250
without slicing himself open,

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:33.950
and melt into the
anonymity of the street."

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:38.793
Now, When I first heard about this crime,

00:19:42.688 --> 00:19:44.670
the two big questions

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:48.780
that I began with in my
investigation were these.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:52.900
One, what was this collection,
at the British Museum,

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:54.270
the second largest in the world,

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:57.360
they have 750,000 bird skins,

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:01.070
they have miles of birds
on shelves and spirits,

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:02.580
hundreds of thousands of eggs.

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:03.680
what were they doing there?

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:04.890
I didn't know anything about...

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.870
I thought all there was with
the Natural History Museum

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.680
was just what you see
displayed in the cases

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.173
on your school field trips,

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:15.700
were they, as the feather thief claimed,

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.030
just sitting in some
basement gathering dust,

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:19.830
or did they have some other purpose?

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:21.670
And the second question is,

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:25.010
what on earth possessed Edwin Rist,

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:26.340
a 20-year-old American,

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:30.143
to scale the wall that night
and to steal them, to what end?

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:34.020
The best way to answer that first question

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:35.920
is to introduce you to this guy.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:36.920
And I gather there are a lot

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.400
of ornithologists tuned in today,

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:42.210
so they'll know him right away,

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:44.013
but this is Alfred Russel Wallace.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.020
The best way to get into this.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:53.652
This was someone who...

00:20:53.652 --> 00:20:56.640
There're shelves sagging
with biographies of Wallace.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:58.730
So I won't do the whole run through,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:01.230
but the compressed version

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:04.782
is he was inspired by Darwin
and "The Voyage of the Beagle"

00:21:04.782 --> 00:21:09.110
to go out in pursuit of
an answer to the question

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:10.970
that was the definitely
naturalist at the time,

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:13.330
what is the origin of species?

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:17.531
And young Wallace who was
not a highly educated guy,

00:21:17.531 --> 00:21:21.020
saved up his money and
booked passage to Brazil,

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:24.220
where he spent four years in
intense privation building,

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:25.723
a collection of specimens.

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:32.290
Birds, beetles, sketches, fish skeletons,

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:34.940
like everything he could

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:36.520
because the thinking was

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.290
that you go and build this collection up

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:39.140
and bring it back home

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:42.000
and spend the next part of your career

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.350
closely evaluating these specimens

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:47.460
and looking for points of divergence

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.003
and what info you might make.

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:51.690
And after four years,

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:54.060
he loads all of these things up on a boat

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:57.680
and about a week or so
into the voyage back home,

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:01.230
he's woken by a ship mate telling him

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:02.953
that the ship is on fire.

00:22:03.856 --> 00:22:06.330
And he scampers up to the
deck and he can see the smoke

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.440
curling up through the planks of the ship,

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.720
and he knows everything is gone.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:14.563
He had something like 10,000 bird skins,

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:20.200
like, it would've been a
career establishing collection.

00:22:23.295 --> 00:22:27.210
As the crewmen were jumping
onto the life rafts,

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:30.570
he scampered down and grabbed
a couple of his notebooks

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:34.340
and a pocket watch and a
sextant and barely escaped.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:37.133
But he returned to
London threadbare, broke,

00:22:37.133 --> 00:22:40.590
and with really not much to his name.

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:43.720
But astonishingly, he wrote his way

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:45.420
into the scientific establishment

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.040
that published two books
within the next 18 months,

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:49.920
the number of journal articles,

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:54.150
and was sort of invited into
a lot of these royal societies

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:57.680
who then backed his next expedition,

00:22:57.680 --> 00:23:01.403
which was roughly eight years
in the Malay archipelago.

00:23:03.537 --> 00:23:07.043
Now, while he was there
in the Malay archipelago,

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:10.510
he was the first...

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:14.250
Oops, where is there we go.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:21.430
Western naturalists to lay eyes on this.

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:24.063
This is the king bird-of-paradise.

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:28.210
And it was an astonishing thing to him.

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:33.210
He described this as having
an other worldly beauty,

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:37.450
an intense cinnabar red
body, a rich orange head,

00:23:37.450 --> 00:23:40.870
deep metallic green spots above the eyes.

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:44.250
And he was overcome, Wallace wrote that.

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:45.083
He said,

00:23:45.083 --> 00:23:46.470
"I thought of the long ages of the past

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:48.450
during which the successive generations

00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:50.140
of this little creature
had run their course,

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:51.590
year by year being born

00:23:51.590 --> 00:23:55.170
and living and dying amid
these dark and gloomy woods

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:57.900
with no intelligent eye to
gaze upon their loveliness.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:01.580
To all appearances, such a
want and waste of beauty."

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:04.749
But then Wallace recognized

00:24:04.749 --> 00:24:07.910
that he understood what the threat was

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:08.949
of what he described as,

00:24:08.949 --> 00:24:13.949
if civilized man were to
ever discover these birds,

00:24:14.060 --> 00:24:15.630
that he would come charging in

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.920
and basically disrupt the conditions,

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:22.307
the world that gave rise to
this beauty and kill it off.

00:24:22.307 --> 00:24:24.650
And he wrote that this consideration

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:26.690
must surely tell us that all living things

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:28.183
were not made for man.

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:33.520
And one thing to notice,
this is an extraordinary.

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:35.680
I feel embarrassed describing any birds

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:36.530
in front of Rick here,

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:39.530
but just pay attention
to those tail feathers,

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.290
'cause they'll come up in a moment here.

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:45.139
But these are these sort of Jade discs

00:24:45.139 --> 00:24:50.139
that in the mating display,

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:51.550
the kingbird-of-paradise

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:53.560
will kind of wobble these
things back and forth,

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:56.083
and it's an extraordinary
thing to witness.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:03.750
Now, I'm not gonna get into
the whole extraordinary story,

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:06.020
but of what happened with Wallace.

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:09.740
But while he is there, he
basically contracts malaria

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:14.240
and in a malarial fever pieces together,

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:17.350
and the theory of evolution
through natural selection,

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:19.900
he's impatiently waiting
for his fever to break,

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:23.950
and he writes the first paper
intended for publication

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:27.750
to proffer an answer to this question.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:30.680
And he puts it in the mail and
sends it to Charles Darwin.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.810
I'm not suggesting that Darwin stole it,

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:35.980
but it did spur Darwin
into kind of accelerating

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:39.253
his own publication schedule
of the origin of species.

00:25:40.547 --> 00:25:45.547
Now Wallace returned to London
with his collection in intact

00:25:48.410 --> 00:25:51.910
and sets out out to write
this incredible book

00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:53.140
called, "The Malay Archipelago"

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:54.900
which you should read if you haven't.

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:57.680
But I just want to touch
back on his premonition

00:25:57.680 --> 00:26:00.740
of what would happen if "Civilized men"

00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:02.400
would discover these birds

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:06.320
and that threat came
quicker than he anticipated

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:09.363
in the form of this.

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:16.950
The Victorian feather fever.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.210
If there is such a
thing as a patient zero,

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:20.937
we think it's Marie Antoinette,

00:26:20.937 --> 00:26:25.937
who wedged a diamond-encrusted
egret feather into her hair

00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:31.200
and set off this kind of global arms race

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:34.600
where women were competing to show status

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:37.590
by wearing feathers in their hair

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:41.673
or entire bird skins
mounted to their hats.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:48.200
This set off a worldwide destruction.

00:26:48.250 --> 00:26:51.230
It seemed that whatever
species was in Vogue that month

00:26:51.230 --> 00:26:54.563
would be pushed to the brink
of extra patient, at least.

00:26:57.788 --> 00:26:58.880
By one historians account,

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:01.203
it's the single largest
direct extermination

00:27:01.203 --> 00:27:05.200
of wildlife by humans.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.573
And it was to feed this marketplace,

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:13.970
which was itself inflamed by
new innovations and mass media

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.590
like rotary printing presses and magazines

00:27:17.590 --> 00:27:20.310
that were pushing these
images throughout the world,

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:23.320
that the inaugural cover
of Vogue, for example,

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:26.083
has a woman with feathers in her hat.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:29.680
You can see an example here.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:31.240
I mean, it might be hard to see,

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:34.190
but on the left, that's a
photo of an auction lot.

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:37.320
And I think there's 1,000
hummingbirds for sale there?

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:40.900
Some of the things that I came across

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:42.890
while researching this
book were horrifying,

00:27:42.890 --> 00:27:46.140
I mean, coats that were
being sold that had...

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:47.160
Well, there was one shawl

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:51.313
that was made of 7,500 hummingbird
skins stitched together.

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:55.890
On the right was a kind of like, I mean,

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:57.790
it's from "Punch Magazine"

00:27:57.790 --> 00:28:01.930
which is a kind of satirical publication,

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:04.190
but criticizing women's fashion

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:09.190
for leading to the
destruction of bird life.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:15.540
But less did seemed that
I'm kind of pointing

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:17.700
a damning finger at women.

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:20.030
It was men doing much of the killing,

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:22.900
and it was also women that led the charge

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:24.190
to pull us back from the brink.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:28.420
It was women that revitalized
a more dollar bond society.

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:29.420
It was women that founded

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:31.763
the Royal Society for
the Protection of Birds.

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:36.750
And both of these have pledges

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:40.720
that their members have to take an oath,

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:45.720
not to wear feathers
or to wear bird skins.

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:48.077
And this was a sort of revitalization

00:28:48.077 --> 00:28:50.270
or some people have argued,

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:54.260
like a new birth of the
conservation movement

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:56.200
was to pull us back from bird slaughter.

00:28:56.200 --> 00:29:00.750
The first wildlife refuge
shut up in the United States

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:04.470
was to cut down on snowy egret poachers

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:06.720
in the Florida Everglades.

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:09.450
At that time, in the early 1900s,

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.050
an egret plumes were worth

00:29:12.050 --> 00:29:14.400
something like four times
their weight in gold.

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:19.040
Now, Alfred Russell Wallace was no dummy.

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:19.873
He understood

00:29:19.873 --> 00:29:24.873
that with the industrialized
slaughter of bird life

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:26.420
playing out across the planet,

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:30.340
he began to implore the British Museum

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:34.710
to protect his specimens and others,

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:38.583
but to maintain a collection
for future generations.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:45.680
He described these birds
as the individual letters

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:49.250
that make up the words and the sentences,

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:51.870
and ultimately the volumes
of the Earth's deep history.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:54.440
And if we allow them to be destroyed,

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:57.600
then we're basically blinding
ourselves to the past.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:29:59.770
Upon his death, in 1913,

00:30:03.830 --> 00:30:08.830
the British Museum added his
specimens to their collection,

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:14.280
but the threat to them
commenced that immediately,

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:18.310
and World War I, there
were 200,000 pounds of

00:30:18.310 --> 00:30:21.860
thermite bombs dropped by
German Zeppelins over London.

00:30:21.860 --> 00:30:24.550
And World War II, there
were 28 direct hits

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:27.350
on the Natural History Museum in London,

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:32.120
which prompted the
curators then to marshal

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:34.180
a fleet of unmarked trucks

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:38.700
to basically secret these
birds out in the dead of night,

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:43.073
to a countryside museum
in the town of Tring,

00:30:43.993 --> 00:30:48.254
where they were assumed to finally be safe

00:30:48.254 --> 00:30:50.040
from outside threat.

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:52.690
Wallace understood that these specimens

00:30:52.690 --> 00:30:54.530
likely held answers to questions

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:56.950
that scientists hadn't
even thought of yet.

00:30:56.950 --> 00:30:58.680
And so the stakes here were huge.

00:31:00.329 --> 00:31:05.329
No one could have imagined
that after surviving Hitler,

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.300
that the ultimate threat to these birds

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:14.300
would come from an American
fly tire in the 21st century.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:17.670
And this leads us to the second question.

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:20.417
So what prompted Edwin to
climb the wall that night

00:31:20.417 --> 00:31:21.600
and to steal these things?

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:26.244
And the quickest way to
show you that is to do...

00:31:26.244 --> 00:31:29.390
This is like a compressed video here,

00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:31.920
but this is what we're talking about.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:34.183
This is a Victorian salmon fly.

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:39.633
These things can take upwards
of eight plus hours to tie,

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:44.190
and they are tied according to these

00:31:45.980 --> 00:31:50.980
19th century books that
have kind of are revered as,

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:55.783
and almost sort of
monastic religious levels

00:31:57.390 --> 00:32:00.890
in terms of like the adherence
to the particular recipes

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:02.680
that these flies have.

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:05.040
At the same time that women
were showing off their status

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:09.160
by wearing these exotic
birds and their hats,

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:12.256
their aristocratic husbands
were plucking feathers off

00:32:12.256 --> 00:32:16.540
and tying them into these
admittedly beautiful patterns

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:20.550
into salmon flies, which
took on these fancy names,

00:32:20.550 --> 00:32:23.070
like the Jock Scott, and
the Green Highlander,

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:24.993
and the Evangeline, and exordium.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:29.290
Now, it's worth mentioning here

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:33.380
that what we know of salmon now

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:36.450
is that they're essentially color blind

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:38.223
when they're spawning.

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:41.180
You don't have to be an
evolutionary biologist

00:32:41.180 --> 00:32:46.180
to piece together that
there's no earthly reason

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:48.750
why a salmon in Scotland

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:51.360
would ever come across a
bird-of-paradise feather

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:54.000
from the highlands of New Guinea.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:58.990
But this was essentially an art form

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:03.650
that was hugely popular
in the 19th century,

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:07.657
kind of slipped into a very near oblivion

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:11.882
in the 20th century until
the birth of the internet,

00:33:11.882 --> 00:33:16.882
at which point, these books,
resurfaced and eBay came,

00:33:17.077 --> 00:33:19.960
and a lot of people started
finding these women's hats

00:33:19.960 --> 00:33:21.750
in their grandma's attic.

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:24.830
And all of a sudden these birds
that they hadn't seen before

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.053
were available to buy.

00:33:28.649 --> 00:33:32.870
And so this then brings
us to young Edwin Rist.

00:33:35.230 --> 00:33:40.230
Born in Manhattan,
raised in Hudson Valley.

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:42.630
He was 10 years old

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:45.200
when he was walking through
his parents' living room,

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:47.773
when his dad who was a
freelance journalist,

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:51.560
was right at piece for popular mechanics

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:54.633
on the physics of casting a fly.

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:58.803
And he was watching an
Orvis sort of 101 video,

00:33:58.803 --> 00:34:02.998
and Edwin walked through room

00:34:02.998 --> 00:34:07.670
when they were demonstrating
how to tie a simple trout fly.

00:34:07.670 --> 00:34:11.260
And there's a point in the
process where there's a technique

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:12.840
called Paul Marine hackleburg,

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:15.100
where you take a feather,
you tie it to the hook.

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:20.100
And then you can take an
ordinary chicken feather,

00:34:20.180 --> 00:34:22.980
and when you coil it around
the shank of the hook,

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:25.220
every one of those
little fibers splays out,

00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:28.320
and it turns something
that's very ordinary

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:31.530
looking into something that
is extraordinary looking.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:35.370
And for trout fishing, where
you're actually tying flies

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:39.420
that are made to resemble the insect life

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:41.620
in and around the river,

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:43.200
there's a purpose to this.

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:45.290
Those feathers could be used.

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:47.020
These are not exotic or rare feathers.

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:49.360
Like I said, they're Turkey or chicken,

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:54.080
but they might be used to
help the fly stay afloat

00:34:54.080 --> 00:34:55.330
on the surface of the river

00:34:55.330 --> 00:34:57.887
to mimic the legs of the insect.

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:05.615
But Edwin was just unsocial by this

00:35:05.615 --> 00:35:10.390
and raced to the basement,
found some fishing hooks,

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:11.760
ran to his parents' bedroom

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:14.169
and pulled some feathers
from his mom's pillow

00:35:14.169 --> 00:35:16.693
and started trying to tie his own flies.

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:21.330
He was at the time a
already a virtuoso flattest.

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:23.950
And so in between studying

00:35:26.445 --> 00:35:28.940
and practicing the flute,

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:32.050
he became an obsessive trout fly tire.

00:35:32.050 --> 00:35:35.100
And his parents who
were homeschooling him,

00:35:35.100 --> 00:35:40.100
would take him to these
tournaments throughout New England.

00:35:41.870 --> 00:35:43.013
I've been to them,

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:48.810
if they sound a little nerdy, they are.

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:52.850
And I say this as someone
who is a fly fisherman,

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:55.185
but they're a picture large ballrooms

00:35:55.185 --> 00:35:59.770
with tons of booths of
celebrity fly tires,

00:35:59.770 --> 00:36:02.003
who are all hunched over little vices,

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:06.180
tying flies, and Edwin would compete

00:36:06.180 --> 00:36:08.870
where they would tell you,

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:10.830
you need to tie as many
of these flies as you can

00:36:10.830 --> 00:36:12.610
within an hour.

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:14.040
And he started mopping up

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:15.840
and winning first prize, first prize.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:18.470
And it was at one of these events

00:36:18.470 --> 00:36:23.470
that he first came across
these Victorian salmon flies.

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:28.033
And as soon as he saw them,
he fell in love with them,

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:31.160
realized that he no longer wanted to tie

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:33.950
these sort of ugly looking trout flies.

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:34.783
He wanted to tie

00:36:34.783 --> 00:36:38.210
these extraordinarily
beautiful salmon flies.

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:43.100
And he started getting private tutoring

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:45.653
from a master fly tire up in Maine,

00:36:47.030 --> 00:36:50.500
but he immediately was
confronted with a problem.

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:54.470
This 15-year-old did not
have much disposable income

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:58.230
when compared to the other members

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:03.050
of this sort underground community.

00:37:03.050 --> 00:37:04.950
So a bird would come up on eBay,

00:37:04.950 --> 00:37:07.410
but he was never able to afford it

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:09.520
because there were grown men

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:11.810
spending thousands of dollars on it

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:13.623
for their own feather collection.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:17.510
So, Edwin, despite this,

00:37:17.510 --> 00:37:19.050
I mean, he was able to use

00:37:19.050 --> 00:37:21.560
what that community refers as subs,

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:25.923
but substitute feathers where
their died pheasant or turkey

00:37:27.530 --> 00:37:31.750
or chicken feathers died to
look like the exotic recipes

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:34.080
in these 19th century books.

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:36.140
He got so good at this,

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:39.450
that he was hailed as
the future of fly time

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:44.450
in fly tire magazine, which
to my wife's embarrassment,

00:37:44.497 --> 00:37:47.330
I've been a subscriber too for years now,

00:37:47.330 --> 00:37:50.123
when I first discovered this story.

00:37:51.508 --> 00:37:55.030
But as good as he was his mastery of this

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:58.340
was always defined by a kind of longing

00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:01.140
for what he couldn't afford.

00:38:01.140 --> 00:38:04.640
And so at 18, when he was admitted

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:06.610
to the Royal Academy of Music in London,

00:38:06.610 --> 00:38:08.900
he receives an email right before leaving

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:11.136
from a friend of his and a mentor,

00:38:11.136 --> 00:38:14.940
who said, "Hey, while you're there?

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:17.160
You've gotta get into this collection.

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:19.680
You've gotta see these birds
that will blow your mind.

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:22.550
Do whatever you need
to do, but get in there

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:25.057
and see the Natural History
Museum's collection."

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:30.133
So Edwin lies to the British Museum,

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:33.740
says that he's a photographer

00:38:33.740 --> 00:38:35.080
who needs to take photos

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:37.420
of Alfred Russell
Wallace's birds-of-paradise

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:40.270
on behalf of a friend who's
doing a dissertation on them.

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:46.400
And the museum trusting
him, which to be fair,

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:49.790
I don't condemn them for that.

00:38:49.790 --> 00:38:53.910
These, as Rick can attest, these are...

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:56.250
The curators are stewards
of these collections

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:59.260
and they're there to
advance human knowledge.

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:02.060
They can't exist in a bunker
where nobody can see them,

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:05.490
but that calibration
has be deviled museums

00:39:05.490 --> 00:39:08.090
all over the world of who
gets in and who doesn't,

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:09.990
and how do you assess risk.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:11.608
And it's certainly been complicated

00:39:11.608 --> 00:39:14.750
by the story you're about to hear.

00:39:14.750 --> 00:39:19.040
But he goes to train, signs
his name into the log book,

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:22.363
and he's deposited in front of,

00:39:24.090 --> 00:39:28.190
I think the red-ruffed fruitcrow
was maybe the first species

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:29.940
that he was allowed to photograph,

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.950
and he starts snapping pictures.

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:32.880
But while he is there,

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:34.580
he also starts taking pictures

00:39:34.580 --> 00:39:38.970
of the location of the
cabinets and the windows

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:40.530
and the entry and exit points.

00:39:40.530 --> 00:39:45.530
And he's building a kind of
visual map of the museum.

00:39:47.083 --> 00:39:52.083
Now it took me nearly
four years to get him

00:39:52.910 --> 00:39:54.350
to agree to an interview.

00:39:54.350 --> 00:39:56.080
But in that interview,

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:59.260
I asked him to describe how it felt

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:03.490
when he was inside the sort of private

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:06.110
or non-public part of the British Museum.

00:40:06.110 --> 00:40:07.710
And this is how he described it.

00:40:16.370 --> 00:40:21.370
So you can tell almost
as soon as he's there

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:23.105
encountering these, I mean, for example,

00:40:23.105 --> 00:40:25.890
the Natural History Museum in Tring

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:30.663
had 48 red-ruffed fruitcrows,

00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:33.810
at the time they were selling

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:36.803
for something like $6,000 per skin.

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:41.120
He would pull open tray
after tray of birds

00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:46.120
and see massive,
extraordinary financial value.

00:40:47.140 --> 00:40:52.060
But he also, as a fly tire,
saw the creative potential

00:40:52.060 --> 00:40:54.040
that if he had these things,

00:40:54.040 --> 00:40:56.260
he would be able to tie whatever he wanted

00:40:56.260 --> 00:41:00.040
without having the pressure of scarcity.

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:03.260
So as you heard in the prologue,

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:08.260
but he ends up taking these things

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:11.463
after eight months of plotting this heist.

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:16.050
During those eight months
leading up to the heist,

00:41:16.050 --> 00:41:17.450
he registered edwinrist.com,

00:41:18.450 --> 00:41:21.893
which he set up as a sort
of online marketplace.

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:26.910
He ordered the diamond blade glass cutter

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:28.930
and other on eBay.

00:41:28.930 --> 00:41:31.490
This part just seemed
impossible to be true,

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:36.186
but he created a word document

00:41:36.186 --> 00:41:41.186
which was entitled, "Plan
for Museum Invasion.Doc"

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:45.928
where he sort of built his strategy.

00:41:45.928 --> 00:41:50.740
And then after performing in London,

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:55.740
boarded the train, stole 299
specimens from the museum.

00:41:56.390 --> 00:42:00.456
Of those 48 red-ruffed
fruitcrows, he stole 47 of them.

00:42:00.456 --> 00:42:03.820
The curators think that he just didn't see

00:42:03.820 --> 00:42:06.520
one that was kind of wedged
in the back of the drawer.

00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:10.880
Crucially some of these species

00:42:10.880 --> 00:42:12.090
that the numbers are smaller,

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:13.210
but I think he took...

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:15.233
I have to check,

00:42:15.233 --> 00:42:18.893
but I think he took something
like 17 flame bowerbirds,

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:22.210
which may not seem like
a significant number,

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:23.218
but at the time,

00:42:23.218 --> 00:42:26.080
there was something
like 30 flame bowerbirds

00:42:26.080 --> 00:42:28.963
in all of the research
institutions in the world.

00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:33.310
And in case, there should be obvious,

00:42:33.310 --> 00:42:38.150
but some of these birds have been there

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:40.260
and preserved by this chain of curators

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:43.370
since before the word
scientist was even coined.

00:42:43.370 --> 00:42:48.370
So once you steal a bird
gathered from this forest

00:42:49.590 --> 00:42:51.530
or this island in 1850,

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.550
that bird might still be there now,

00:42:53.550 --> 00:42:57.670
but you can't go and get
another one from 1850.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:42:59.422
I mean, this seems blindingly obvious,

00:42:59.422 --> 00:43:02.110
but the whole point of these collections

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:06.350
is to have these sort of,
almost like genetic snapshots

00:43:06.350 --> 00:43:11.350
of what the species was like back then,

00:43:11.922 --> 00:43:16.350
and so that we can understand
how things are changing.

00:43:16.350 --> 00:43:21.350
So the guard did not...

00:43:21.710 --> 00:43:24.450
Whether he was watching
a soccer match or not,

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:26.920
he did not catch Edwin that night.

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:31.140
He climbed back out, he missed
the train back to London

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:36.140
and spent a very kind of angst
ridden three or four hours

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:40.130
at this tiny train stop

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:44.330
with a million dollars worth
of bed birds in a suitcase,

00:43:44.330 --> 00:43:46.543
terrified that the cops
were coming for him.

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:50.473
Got back to his dorm room,
spread the birds out on the bed,

00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:53.440
told one family member

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:57.170
that he felt like the
richest man in the world

00:43:58.390 --> 00:43:59.520
having these collections,

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:04.100
and then proceeded to
start chopping them up

00:44:04.100 --> 00:44:07.240
and hacking the breast plates,

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:09.890
which are the only thing
that fly tires care about

00:44:09.890 --> 00:44:11.892
of the red-ruffed fruitcrow.

00:44:11.892 --> 00:44:12.767
He would hack those out.

00:44:12.767 --> 00:44:15.090
And he took a series of approaches.

00:44:15.090 --> 00:44:16.540
Sometimes he would sell...

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:21.730
Here's his website, and I have...

00:44:21.730 --> 00:44:26.730
It's especially great to be
able to do this with Yale,

00:44:27.560 --> 00:44:28.393
and with Rick today,

00:44:28.393 --> 00:44:32.343
because when Rick first heard about this,

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:36.110
he had the foresight to
take these screenshots,

00:44:36.110 --> 00:44:39.490
the only ones that exist,
of Edwin's website,

00:44:39.490 --> 00:44:44.070
which were subsequently
obliterated from the web.

00:44:44.070 --> 00:44:46.340
But these are to anyone watching this,

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:49.650
who was a curator or works
with these collections,

00:44:49.650 --> 00:44:51.980
these are obviously studies specimens.

00:44:51.980 --> 00:44:53.570
These are museum specimens

00:44:53.570 --> 00:44:56.060
that he's posting on a public site.

00:44:56.060 --> 00:44:57.990
He's using their Latin binomials.

00:44:57.990 --> 00:45:00.883
He's not even using the
slang for these things.

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:05.360
But he would sell full birds.

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:07.880
One American dentist...

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:09.520
There's a lot of dentists in this story,

00:45:09.520 --> 00:45:12.700
I think it's because dentists are good

00:45:12.700 --> 00:45:16.670
with working with little
tools and tight spaces.

00:45:16.670 --> 00:45:18.910
And so that's my best theory,

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:21.600
but if one American dentist
boarded a plane for London

00:45:21.600 --> 00:45:25.160
and bought something like
$10,000 worth of birds.

00:45:25.160 --> 00:45:27.700
All of these guys that bought from Edwin,

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:28.940
and we're talking about a total

00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:32.330
of like some several $1,000 worth of staff

00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:35.683
that the British authorities
alleged that he sold,

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:41.930
none of them seemed to wonder
or maybe did not want to ask

00:45:42.520 --> 00:45:45.030
how a college student was suddenly sitting

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:48.510
upon the most valuable private collection

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:53.440
of priceless exotic
specimens in the world,

00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:57.440
but they were quite happy
to just do the transactions.

00:45:57.440 --> 00:46:01.900
Now here, you'll see some of
my obsessive screenshotting

00:46:01.900 --> 00:46:02.733
over the years,

00:46:02.733 --> 00:46:05.480
but these are private
Facebook group exchanges

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:08.530
where they're stressing to each other,

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:10.150
that you never talk about the price.

00:46:10.150 --> 00:46:13.420
You only do this over private message.

00:46:13.420 --> 00:46:16.150
These are birds where you can see the tags

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:20.813
which bear all the bio
data tied to their feet.

00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:24.800
He would also...

00:46:27.186 --> 00:46:28.090
Hang on, my screen just froze.

00:46:28.090 --> 00:46:28.923
Here we go.

00:46:28.923 --> 00:46:31.870
He would also post photos
like this to these forums

00:46:34.190 --> 00:46:37.530
where you have to
understand some of these,

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:41.973
that bottom row of feathers,
six of them are $100.

00:46:44.480 --> 00:46:47.930
And I remember asking Rick this,

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:49.580
but I think the breastplate

00:46:49.580 --> 00:46:52.207
has something like
maybe 800 feathers on it

00:46:52.207 --> 00:46:53.670
for a single bird.

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:56.010
And he stole 47 of them.

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:56.910
So you could do the math

00:46:56.910 --> 00:46:58.810
of how quickly this becomes lucrative.

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:04.290
Here, this is July 27th 2010.

00:47:04.290 --> 00:47:09.290
This is before the arrest,

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:12.110
before he was eventually caught,

00:47:12.110 --> 00:47:14.190
the fly time forums that
he would sell these on

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:18.540
are joking about the price
that Edwin is charging

00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:23.330
and the 3000% markup that
he's making on birds stolen

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:25.150
from a natural history museum.

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:28.880
Now, it took the British Museum 35 days

00:47:28.880 --> 00:47:32.398
to realize that they were
actually missing specimens.

00:47:32.398 --> 00:47:34.893
The morning after the heist,

00:47:35.940 --> 00:47:40.940
they raced over to the cabinets
housing Darwin's birds,

00:47:42.835 --> 00:47:45.290
and it's kind of a final blow to Wallace.

00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:48.210
But had they cared about
Wallace as much as Darwin,

00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:52.060
they probably would've checked
Wallace's specimens as well

00:47:52.060 --> 00:47:53.950
and realized that they had been burgled.

00:47:53.950 --> 00:47:56.930
But they concluded within a day or so

00:47:56.930 --> 00:47:59.760
that it was a sort of
failed break and enter

00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:02.454
and basically breed the sigh relief.

00:48:02.454 --> 00:48:04.460
And it wasn't until a month or so later

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:06.566
that a visiting researcher came

00:48:06.566 --> 00:48:09.000
and opened up the trays

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:13.580
that were supposed to be holding
the red-ruffed fruitcrows

00:48:13.580 --> 00:48:14.630
and found them empty.

00:48:14.630 --> 00:48:17.160
And so the museum then did an inventory,

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:20.763
they made the tally,
they announced the heist.

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:24.010
The detective assigned to the case

00:48:24.010 --> 00:48:26.081
did a number of interviews of the staff

00:48:26.081 --> 00:48:28.653
to rule out an inside job.

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:34.960
But the British Museum doesn't
like this characterization.

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:36.560
I can understand why,

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:41.355
but I think they quickly
just came to an assumption

00:48:41.355 --> 00:48:44.743
that the birds had probably
all been chopped up.

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:49.963
For the life of me, I don't understand.

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:52.650
I've been through this many times now,

00:48:52.650 --> 00:48:57.650
but Edwin Rist was emailing
them about the very birds

00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:01.093
that were subsequently stolen.

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:04.993
He signed his real name
into their log book,

00:49:06.870 --> 00:49:08.402
but for whatever reason,

00:49:08.402 --> 00:49:11.450
it took 15 months for law enforcement

00:49:11.450 --> 00:49:13.970
to finally turn their eyes on Edwin.

00:49:13.970 --> 00:49:15.220
And that was not because of...

00:49:15.220 --> 00:49:19.060
With all respect to the
wonderful British detective

00:49:19.060 --> 00:49:21.680
who was running point on this case,

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:23.810
it wasn't because of gum shoe work,

00:49:23.810 --> 00:49:26.360
it was because another police officer

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:35.870
who was not assigned to the case

00:49:35.870 --> 00:49:37.330
but who was an avid fly tire,

00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:40.690
went to a festival in Holland

00:49:40.690 --> 00:49:45.690
where he saw someone showing
off a priceless specimen.

00:49:46.690 --> 00:49:49.970
And he knew about the train heist

00:49:49.970 --> 00:49:51.270
and he started asking questions.

00:49:51.270 --> 00:49:54.720
And so that law enforcement officer

00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:59.540
who I'm obligated to
protect his anonymity,

00:49:59.540 --> 00:50:01.640
but he's the one that
tipped off the authorities

00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:03.323
and that led to Edwin's arrest.

00:50:04.443 --> 00:50:09.250
Now, I wanna get us to Q&amp;A,

00:50:09.250 --> 00:50:12.550
but I don't wanna go through every twist

00:50:12.550 --> 00:50:15.243
and turn of my subsequent investigation,

00:50:15.243 --> 00:50:20.243
but a couple photos here of
the crime scene evidence.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:25.250
So of the 299 birds stolen,

00:50:25.250 --> 00:50:27.340
the police recovered 102 of them

00:50:27.340 --> 00:50:29.580
that were intact with
their labels attached,

00:50:29.580 --> 00:50:31.140
which is a crucial detail.

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:32.750
The labels are cut off.

00:50:32.750 --> 00:50:37.000
They're essentially
meaningless for research

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:39.023
or greatly diminished for research.

00:50:40.530 --> 00:50:43.763
72 were intact, but were
missing their labels.

00:50:44.670 --> 00:50:47.250
And beyond that, they found
a mountain of Ziploc bags

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:49.403
filled with severed parts of birds.

00:50:50.290 --> 00:50:53.220
After the news of the
arrest and other 19 skins,

00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:55.470
all of them missing their labels

00:50:55.470 --> 00:50:58.450
were returned by mail anonymously.

00:50:58.450 --> 00:51:01.090
And as far as the skin still at large,

00:51:01.090 --> 00:51:04.530
about a third of them were still missing.

00:51:04.530 --> 00:51:05.560
And so even though,

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:07.340
and here you'll see
the resplendent castles

00:51:07.340 --> 00:51:10.030
were the tales have been hacked off

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:11.963
and sold off to the community.

00:51:14.380 --> 00:51:17.590
But even though the British
Museum described this

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:20.750
as a catastrophic theft from humanity,

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:22.240
and even though they
still hadn't recovered

00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:23.560
a huge number of skins,

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:25.540
they announced, after the arrest,

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:28.207
that, "Please the matter is resolved."

00:51:30.280 --> 00:51:32.320
When I first heard about this

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:36.100
and then realized that not
only had this really happened,

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:38.570
because it just seemed
so impossibly strange,

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:39.833
but that there was still,

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:44.470
hundreds of thousands of
dollars worth of these skins

00:51:44.470 --> 00:51:49.470
and feathers floating around
this sort of black market,

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:53.160
and that the feather thief

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:55.630
had basically had never
spent a night behind bars,

00:51:55.630 --> 00:51:57.090
and we can get into that in Q&amp;A,

00:51:57.090 --> 00:51:59.750
but he was basically
given a slap on the wrist.

00:51:59.750 --> 00:52:02.870
He was allowed to graduate
from the Royal Academy of Music

00:52:02.870 --> 00:52:04.689
and go on his way.

00:52:04.689 --> 00:52:06.280
That the British authorities

00:52:06.280 --> 00:52:08.700
were no longer looking for the specimens.

00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:11.883
And that I was seen as
I getting deeper into,

00:52:11.883 --> 00:52:14.270
what I kind of jokingly called,
"A feather underground,"

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:17.170
that they were basically just
cracking jokes about it all.

00:52:18.490 --> 00:52:23.490
That it struck me as wrong
on a very basic level.

00:52:24.210 --> 00:52:28.411
And so this was a five
plus year investigation

00:52:28.411 --> 00:52:30.290
where I went all over the world,

00:52:30.290 --> 00:52:31.750
infiltrating this community,

00:52:31.750 --> 00:52:33.433
turning people into sources.

00:52:35.536 --> 00:52:38.436
And I'm happy to discuss
that investigation,

00:52:38.436 --> 00:52:42.773
but among the most
shocking revelations of it,

00:52:43.810 --> 00:52:46.400
I'm now at five separate museum heist

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:48.530
that have been carried out by fly tires

00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:50.023
in the last 20 years.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:54.360
Edwin's was the worst that we know of,

00:52:54.360 --> 00:52:58.560
but it's by no means
an isolated occurrence.

00:52:58.560 --> 00:53:00.300
Even when I launched this book...

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:01.690
The first event launching this book

00:53:01.690 --> 00:53:04.630
was at the Los Angeles
Natural History Museum,

00:53:04.630 --> 00:53:06.060
and shortly before the event,

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:08.340
the head of their bird
collection took me over

00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:09.730
and showed me a cabinet

00:53:09.730 --> 00:53:11.120
and showed me the crowbar marks

00:53:11.120 --> 00:53:12.680
where somebody had pried open a case

00:53:12.680 --> 00:53:15.570
and stolen the same species of birds

00:53:16.480 --> 00:53:20.113
that were targeted in the train heist.

00:53:21.740 --> 00:53:26.133
There's an American fly
tire who was posing.

00:53:27.130 --> 00:53:31.200
He won a pest control contract

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:33.390
with a couple museums in Europe,

00:53:33.390 --> 00:53:36.860
and he would go in at night
and spray for insects.

00:53:36.860 --> 00:53:38.860
And then he would take priceless birds

00:53:38.860 --> 00:53:41.453
into his coveralls and slip out.

00:53:42.690 --> 00:53:43.713
He was never caught.

00:53:45.750 --> 00:53:49.417
So I just wanted to say
that this is a story

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:56.663
that I initially thought
was just kind of a quirky,

00:53:57.530 --> 00:54:01.480
why on earth would people steal birds?

00:54:01.480 --> 00:54:03.480
But it very quickly became something

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:05.870
much more profound about our relationship

00:54:05.870 --> 00:54:10.870
to a changing planet
and vanishing wildlife,

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:13.740
and also the kind of blinders

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:16.800
that obsession can give to people.

00:54:16.800 --> 00:54:18.863
Just to end it on a lighter note,

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:23.530
towards the end of what
was an eight hour interview

00:54:23.530 --> 00:54:25.310
with the feather thief,

00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:28.093
I was trying light in the mood and I said,

00:54:29.020 --> 00:54:31.540
hey, when you go on a date with somebody,

00:54:31.540 --> 00:54:34.930
do you feel like the need
to say, just so like,

00:54:34.930 --> 00:54:36.929
I'm a feather thief, but I'm whatever.

00:54:36.929 --> 00:54:41.929
And I saw his brow furrow
when I used the word, "Thief."

00:54:42.640 --> 00:54:43.960
And I said, well, is that a problem?

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:45.410
Like, you do not like that word,

00:54:45.410 --> 00:54:46.810
and this is how he answered.

00:54:57.100 --> 00:54:57.953
I hope, hopefully you could hear that.

00:54:57.953 --> 00:54:59.850
I dunno if the audio was good enough,

00:54:59.850 --> 00:55:04.760
but I'm not a psychiatrist
or psychologist,

00:55:04.760 --> 00:55:08.490
but I think the term for
that is compartmentalization.

00:55:08.490 --> 00:55:09.323
So, all right.

00:55:09.323 --> 00:55:13.142
So I'm gonna stop my screen share here.

00:55:13.142 --> 00:55:17.880
And I would love to dive into
question if you have any.

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:21.110
But again, I'm really grateful to Rick,

00:55:21.110 --> 00:55:24.480
and to to the Frankie's and
Yale for making this happen.

00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:27.020
And I hope that sets the table enough here

00:55:27.020 --> 00:55:29.800
for us to have a chat.

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:31.360
- Great, thanks so much, Kirk.

00:55:31.360 --> 00:55:35.410
And I'll just say

00:55:35.410 --> 00:55:39.686
that since nobody has to
get in a car and drive home,

00:55:39.686 --> 00:55:43.943
we're gonna let the
questions go for a while.

00:55:44.960 --> 00:55:46.300
Thanks, Kirk.

00:55:46.300 --> 00:55:49.470
It's always striking
to be reminded of this,

00:55:49.470 --> 00:55:51.080
and obviously you documented

00:55:51.080 --> 00:55:54.250
how the worldwide impact
on scientific capacity

00:55:54.250 --> 00:55:58.183
to study this bio diversity.

00:55:59.190 --> 00:56:03.933
I'm really struck by that description,

00:56:05.180 --> 00:56:07.930
or Edwin's description of himself

00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:10.963
as somebody who really
understands the feathers.

00:56:12.640 --> 00:56:14.900
For him, somebody really
understands the feathers

00:56:14.900 --> 00:56:19.900
is what they materially do as
you wrap them around a hook,

00:56:20.180 --> 00:56:24.280
not the role of feathers in
the biology, the bird, the-

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:29.160
- Exactly, and I imagine you probably

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:31.140
were pulling your hair out

00:56:32.520 --> 00:56:33.910
when you got to this point in the book,

00:56:33.910 --> 00:56:38.860
but I mean, he was pushing a
mountain of just junk science

00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:40.390
in the interview where he said,

00:56:40.390 --> 00:56:43.608
essentially after 100 years,

00:56:43.608 --> 00:56:46.950
you can't really pull any
DNA from them anymore,

00:56:46.950 --> 00:56:50.430
and all the real value

00:56:50.430 --> 00:56:53.140
is really just in the height and weight

00:56:53.140 --> 00:56:54.000
and that's kind of it.

00:56:54.000 --> 00:56:56.190
So there were a lot of points

00:56:56.190 --> 00:56:59.830
where I had kind your...

00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:03.437
As you described it, many
graduate seminar I had

00:57:03.437 --> 00:57:04.390
during the interview,

00:57:04.390 --> 00:57:07.050
where I wanted to say, are you nuts?

00:57:07.050 --> 00:57:12.050
But I wanted to just let
him sort of hang himself

00:57:12.760 --> 00:57:14.640
with these comments, does that make sense?

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:16.460
- Yeah, well, we have
lots of great questions.

00:57:16.460 --> 00:57:18.420
I just remind people,
if you have questions,

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:20.773
put 'em in the Q&amp;A we're monitoring it.

00:57:22.600 --> 00:57:26.790
First from Katie, is
Rist still tying flies?

00:57:26.790 --> 00:57:27.960
- Oh, that's a great question.

00:57:27.960 --> 00:57:32.460
One that I don't think I
can't answer definitively,

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:34.941
but because as you might imagine,

00:57:34.941 --> 00:57:38.643
he and I are not really
