00:59:20.510 --> 00:59:24.270
Now, at some point in this investigation,

00:59:24.270 --> 00:59:28.030
I was basically just cold
calling and cold emailing people

00:59:28.030 --> 00:59:29.647
within the agency saying,

00:59:29.647 --> 00:59:31.770
"Why aren't you guys
paying attention to this?"

00:59:31.770 --> 00:59:34.680
Because I was on eBay constantly.

00:59:34.680 --> 00:59:38.049
I was pulling so many records

00:59:38.049 --> 00:59:43.049
of sales of sites
protected species on eBay

00:59:43.080 --> 00:59:45.000
that I had to hire an assistant

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:47.750
to basically build this Excel sheet.

00:59:47.750 --> 00:59:50.280
And I shared that with Fish and Wildlife,

00:59:50.280 --> 00:59:52.600
and it was only then that they...

00:59:52.600 --> 00:59:55.370
I can say all this now,
I'll explain it in a second.

00:59:55.370 --> 00:59:58.190
But they finally called me

00:59:58.190 --> 01:00:01.129
and said that they were opening
an investigation into this.

01:00:01.129 --> 01:00:03.880
That it was going to
be like an active case

01:00:03.880 --> 01:00:06.340
with an agent or two assigned to it,

01:00:06.340 --> 01:00:08.791
and would I sign on as a
confidential informant.

01:00:08.791 --> 01:00:10.566
I can say that because I'm the least

01:00:10.566 --> 01:00:13.140
like if there were crackdowns,

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:16.200
like, who do you think the guy
was that tipped off the feds?

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:19.040
Like, it's pretty obvious that it was me,

01:00:19.040 --> 01:00:20.930
but the timing of it is crucial

01:00:20.930 --> 01:00:24.433
because that phone call
happened in December of 2016,

01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:30.760
which was the penultimate month

01:00:30.760 --> 01:00:32.070
of the Obama administration.

01:00:32.070 --> 01:00:33.424
And so then when Trump came in

01:00:33.424 --> 01:00:37.050
the kind of fledgling case

01:00:37.050 --> 01:00:39.070
just sort of was closed after that.

01:00:39.070 --> 01:00:43.133
So it's not a priority of theirs.

01:00:44.380 --> 01:00:47.063
And the question of how
Edwin got away with it,

01:00:49.719 --> 01:00:51.227
it's a big part of the book,

01:00:51.227 --> 01:00:55.250
but essentially his lawyers got him

01:00:55.250 --> 01:00:58.030
in front of Bohert's cousin,

01:00:58.030 --> 01:01:01.513
Simon Baron-Cohen, who is
Sacha Baron Cohen's cousin,

01:01:02.570 --> 01:01:07.570
who is one of the leading
experts on Asperger's.

01:01:10.410 --> 01:01:14.730
And over the course of one
hour or so assessment of Edwin,

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:17.270
he concluded that Edwin had Asperger's

01:01:17.270 --> 01:01:21.470
and therefore didn't really
realize what he was doing.

01:01:21.470 --> 01:01:24.710
Now, I've had a lot of people write to me

01:01:24.710 --> 01:01:28.700
that Asperger's doesn't even exist

01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:30.850
as a standalone diagnosis anymore,

01:01:30.850 --> 01:01:32.170
but that people who are on the spectrum

01:01:32.170 --> 01:01:34.590
often have a heightened
awareness of right and wrong.

01:01:34.590 --> 01:01:37.110
That just because you're on the spectrum

01:01:37.110 --> 01:01:39.100
doesn't mean that you don't recognize

01:01:39.100 --> 01:01:41.960
that plotting a museum heist for a year

01:01:41.960 --> 01:01:44.260
and stealing things that
aren't yours is a wrong thing.

01:01:44.260 --> 01:01:45.750
It's a, kind of, an offensive thing.

01:01:45.750 --> 01:01:50.750
But the brits, they were able
to basically cite a series

01:01:50.990 --> 01:01:53.380
of precedents in British case law,

01:01:53.380 --> 01:01:56.380
where people with an
assessment of Asperger's

01:01:56.380 --> 01:01:59.430
were then not ever sent to prison.

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:03.100
And obviously Americans, we
wanna throw everyone in prison,

01:02:03.100 --> 01:02:04.700
so I'm not advocating that,

01:02:04.700 --> 01:02:08.580
but he truly was not punished.

01:02:08.580 --> 01:02:11.480
He had a confiscation order

01:02:11.480 --> 01:02:14.614
for something like $10,000 or $15,000,

01:02:14.614 --> 01:02:17.280
and then he was sent on his way.

01:02:17.280 --> 01:02:21.103
So there really was no
meaningful punishment here.

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:25.400
- Yeah, one user asked,

01:02:25.400 --> 01:02:26.860
how much pushback have you gotten

01:02:26.860 --> 01:02:31.353
from the fly tying community
since the publication?

01:02:32.290 --> 01:02:33.123
- Well, it's interesting

01:02:33.123 --> 01:02:38.123
because one thing I neglected to mention

01:02:38.380 --> 01:02:40.870
is that the overwhelming majority

01:02:40.870 --> 01:02:43.721
of the men who tie salmon flies,

01:02:43.721 --> 01:02:47.740
and I say men, because I
spent years researching this,

01:02:47.740 --> 01:02:50.040
and I did not find a
single woman who was tying.

01:02:50.040 --> 01:02:51.420
I got excited, 'cause at one point

01:02:51.420 --> 01:02:55.760
I saw a woman posting flies on Facebook,

01:02:55.760 --> 01:02:58.550
but it was just her
husband using her account.

01:02:58.550 --> 01:02:59.430
But overwhelmingly,

01:02:59.430 --> 01:03:02.800
the men that tie the
Victorian salmon flies

01:03:02.800 --> 01:03:04.110
do not fish at all.

01:03:04.110 --> 01:03:06.390
They don't know how to fish,
they don't own the gear.

01:03:06.390 --> 01:03:08.740
This is just an art form for them.

01:03:08.740 --> 01:03:10.060
And I mean, quite honestly,

01:03:10.060 --> 01:03:14.653
you'd have to be a lunatic
to cast a $2,000 worth

01:03:15.890 --> 01:03:17.560
of feathers into a river

01:03:17.560 --> 01:03:20.440
when you can catch salmon
with dog for tied to a hook.

01:03:20.440 --> 01:03:24.560
I mean, I had some one angler
right to me with a photo

01:03:24.560 --> 01:03:28.100
of a great salmon that he caught with...

01:03:28.100 --> 01:03:31.261
He had run out of flies and
he tied a candy bar rapper

01:03:31.261 --> 01:03:33.190
to his hook and caught one.

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:34.320
Salmon aren't easy to catch,

01:03:34.320 --> 01:03:36.180
but they're not drawn

01:03:36.180 --> 01:03:37.800
to these particular feathers, obviously.

01:03:37.800 --> 01:03:42.800
So there's a very pronounced dichotomy,

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:45.170
the ones who don't know how to fish

01:03:45.170 --> 01:03:48.130
and who tie these salmon
flies, they hate me,

01:03:48.130 --> 01:03:51.700
they hate the book, "The Fly Fishermen,"

01:03:51.700 --> 01:03:53.710
the guide that first tipped me off

01:03:53.710 --> 01:03:56.540
to this story 10 years ago in New Mexico,

01:03:56.540 --> 01:03:58.526
has twice had to go to
local law enforcement

01:03:58.526 --> 01:04:00.643
for a specific death threats

01:04:00.643 --> 01:04:02.850
that he's received in this community

01:04:02.850 --> 01:04:05.920
for the sin of telling me about this story

01:04:05.920 --> 01:04:06.943
in the first place,

01:04:08.330 --> 01:04:10.100
but the other, the rest of the community,

01:04:10.100 --> 01:04:15.100
which are actual anglers and
people who know how to fish

01:04:15.740 --> 01:04:17.580
and who are using trout flies

01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:21.380
and who have a kind of maybe
a more realistic awareness

01:04:21.380 --> 01:04:23.883
and appreciation of nature
and of conservation,

01:04:25.090 --> 01:04:27.850
they all love the book and celebrate.

01:04:27.850 --> 01:04:30.560
But my biggest antagonists with this book

01:04:30.560 --> 01:04:34.070
have been classic salmon fly
tires and the British Museum,

01:04:34.070 --> 01:04:35.816
which is one of the only museums on earth

01:04:35.816 --> 01:04:38.530
that just won't carry the book.

01:04:38.530 --> 01:04:40.423
You can't find it in their gift shop.

01:04:40.423 --> 01:04:42.630
So yeah, it's a strange...

01:04:42.630 --> 01:04:44.600
I think the British Museum never...

01:04:45.620 --> 01:04:49.370
They weren't able to step
past their embarrassment

01:04:49.370 --> 01:04:53.000
to recognize that this entire
book was a love letter to them

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:55.510
and to their mission and
to their institution.

01:04:55.510 --> 01:04:57.440
And that I spent all of this
time going around the world,

01:04:57.440 --> 01:04:58.700
trying to track down these skins

01:04:58.700 --> 01:05:01.400
so that they could be
returned to that museum

01:05:01.400 --> 01:05:04.340
for the proper use of these specimens.

01:05:04.340 --> 01:05:08.110
But I think they were more concerned

01:05:08.110 --> 01:05:11.220
with what they call reputational damage.

01:05:11.220 --> 01:05:13.697
- Yeah, we have a question from Raquel,

01:05:13.697 --> 01:05:17.860
the disrespectful attitude of
the fly time community aside,

01:05:17.860 --> 01:05:19.920
is there an argument to
be made that fly tires

01:05:19.920 --> 01:05:21.450
also have valid appreciation

01:05:21.450 --> 01:05:24.140
of these specimens for the
purpose of art and beauty

01:05:24.140 --> 01:05:25.500
if not for scientific knowledge?

01:05:25.500 --> 01:05:28.291
What if the museum were to
sell some of their extras

01:05:28.291 --> 01:05:31.340
once the scientific
data had been collected?

01:05:31.340 --> 01:05:33.638
- I think you should answer that one.

01:05:33.638 --> 01:05:37.250
- Well, yes.

01:05:37.250 --> 01:05:38.340
Well, the interesting thing

01:05:38.340 --> 01:05:41.670
is that we never reach an end result

01:05:41.670 --> 01:05:45.130
of when we've collected all the data,

01:05:45.130 --> 01:05:48.140
because in fact, obviously
DNA wasn't described

01:05:48.140 --> 01:05:53.140
until nearly 100 years after
Wallace wrote his paper

01:06:00.870 --> 01:06:03.200
or collected many of these specimens.

01:06:03.200 --> 01:06:05.180
And now, and in fact, we can still,

01:06:05.180 --> 01:06:06.550
and we do get better and better

01:06:06.550 --> 01:06:10.728
at acquiring lots of useful
DNA specimens of the things.

01:06:10.728 --> 01:06:13.140
So that notion is wrong.

01:06:13.140 --> 01:06:14.550
And the other part about it

01:06:14.550 --> 01:06:19.550
is that we are incredibly highly regulated

01:06:19.720 --> 01:06:23.590
to make collections today

01:06:23.590 --> 01:06:25.270
requires lots and lots of permits,

01:06:25.270 --> 01:06:26.830
which have to go through lots and lots

01:06:26.830 --> 01:06:29.630
of ongoing regulation.

01:06:29.630 --> 01:06:34.630
And so those permits would not be given

01:06:37.700 --> 01:06:39.180
for the purposes of making art.

01:06:39.180 --> 01:06:42.160
And indeed, one of the
interesting things about this

01:06:42.160 --> 01:06:47.160
is it's roots in Victorian
mark until colonialism,

01:06:49.180 --> 01:06:50.310
though all those feathers,

01:06:50.310 --> 01:06:53.860
all those earls who were
inventing these flies

01:06:53.860 --> 01:06:56.460
back in the Victorian
era were buying these

01:06:56.460 --> 01:06:59.470
because they were available
from all of the colonies

01:06:59.470 --> 01:07:01.570
that had have been gathered.

01:07:01.570 --> 01:07:05.520
So it's really a return to that
kind of concept of the world

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:08.360
that allows this art form to exist.

01:07:08.360 --> 01:07:11.293
And some of forms auto go extinct.

01:07:12.140 --> 01:07:12.973
- And I think...

01:07:12.973 --> 01:07:14.520
I was just looking at one of the comments,

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:18.520
but like initially

01:07:18.520 --> 01:07:22.560
when I knew nothing
about any of this stuff,

01:07:22.560 --> 01:07:24.250
I thought, well, isn't
there something to it?

01:07:24.250 --> 01:07:25.780
I remember talking to
you about this, Rick.

01:07:25.780 --> 01:07:27.760
But that here are guys

01:07:27.760 --> 01:07:31.130
who really love and
appreciate these birds.

01:07:31.130 --> 01:07:34.130
But it quickly...

01:07:34.130 --> 01:07:39.130
Their appreciation for
them is in a kind of,

01:07:42.030 --> 01:07:43.710
I've used the word to deliberately,

01:07:43.710 --> 01:07:47.160
but it's in a cult like appreciation

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:52.160
where there are certain series of flies

01:07:52.730 --> 01:07:54.970
that are supposed to only be tied

01:07:54.970 --> 01:07:56.730
with a particular type of feather.

01:07:56.730 --> 01:07:58.970
And when you go deep into all of this,

01:07:58.970 --> 01:08:00.070
these guys talk about

01:08:00.070 --> 01:08:04.160
the kind of exquisite
quality of how this feather

01:08:04.160 --> 01:08:06.430
can be married to another feather

01:08:06.430 --> 01:08:09.650
and the way that the
recess bends differently

01:08:09.650 --> 01:08:12.603
than a chicken feather would and all this,

01:08:13.980 --> 01:08:16.680
there's zero science to any of it.

01:08:16.680 --> 01:08:20.330
And so that's where I felt
like anyone, myself included,

01:08:20.330 --> 01:08:23.370
like at some point you
have to pick a side here.

01:08:23.370 --> 01:08:25.940
Like, is this just an aesthetic pursuit

01:08:25.940 --> 01:08:30.940
and beauty for beauty's
sake or do these things,

01:08:30.983 --> 01:08:35.050
should they be used for the
advancement of human knowledge?

01:08:35.050 --> 01:08:38.183
I very clearly ended
up in the latter camp,

01:08:39.450 --> 01:08:40.440
but yeah.

01:08:40.440 --> 01:08:43.300
- There's so many great questions here.

01:08:43.300 --> 01:08:44.550
We won't get to all of them,

01:08:44.550 --> 01:08:47.487
but I just wanna highlight,
we have Guy Curwen

01:08:50.670 --> 01:08:53.734
author of "Mannequins
and Egos of the world"

01:08:53.734 --> 01:08:56.020
monograph of the family.

01:08:56.020 --> 01:08:59.090
Do you think that Rist does
not consider himself a thief

01:08:59.090 --> 01:09:00.693
because he wasn't incarcerated?

01:09:05.710 --> 01:09:08.110
- I don't know how to
answer that, to be honest.

01:09:11.036 --> 01:09:15.500
I think he still believes
that these specimens

01:09:15.500 --> 01:09:20.500
have no rightful place in museums

01:09:21.590 --> 01:09:24.460
and that it's the curators,

01:09:24.460 --> 01:09:26.740
and in fact, you know, the South African

01:09:26.740 --> 01:09:31.240
that I opened this talk
with admitted to me,

01:09:31.240 --> 01:09:33.100
and then on the record interview

01:09:33.100 --> 01:09:36.140
that he still has many of these birds

01:09:36.140 --> 01:09:37.250
that he bought from Edwin,

01:09:37.250 --> 01:09:39.980
but he refuses to send them
back to the British Museum

01:09:41.270 --> 01:09:42.920
because he doesn't care.

01:09:42.920 --> 01:09:44.280
But also because he believes

01:09:44.280 --> 01:09:47.100
that these curators are
doing the devil's work

01:09:47.100 --> 01:09:49.030
and that they're pushing evolution

01:09:49.030 --> 01:09:52.180
and all of this sort of
Satan stuff in his mind.

01:09:52.180 --> 01:09:53.020
And also he knows

01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:58.020
that this is not gonna rise to the level

01:09:58.130 --> 01:10:00.634
of like an Interpol
international operation

01:10:00.634 --> 01:10:02.840
to go and reclaim these things,

01:10:02.840 --> 01:10:06.963
especially if the British
Museum is not clamoring for it.

01:10:07.810 --> 01:10:12.510
And so I think if you discount

01:10:12.510 --> 01:10:15.270
the value of the thing that you stole,

01:10:15.270 --> 01:10:18.260
then I think it makes
it easier to discount

01:10:20.750 --> 01:10:22.107
your own culpability in it.

01:10:22.107 --> 01:10:25.140
And so that's my best
guess at why he could say,

01:10:25.140 --> 01:10:26.540
he's not a thief.

01:10:26.540 --> 01:10:27.650
- Yeah. Yeah.

01:10:27.650 --> 01:10:28.740
Well, last question.

01:10:28.740 --> 01:10:31.160
I've lost it in feedback,

01:10:31.160 --> 01:10:35.390
but do you worry that this

01:10:35.390 --> 01:10:39.727
may actually encourage future thefts?

01:10:42.600 --> 01:10:45.660
- Yeah, it certainly crossed my mind,

01:10:45.660 --> 01:10:49.300
but I will say that one of
the consequences of this book

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:53.320
has been, and I see that
there's a lot of curators

01:10:53.320 --> 01:10:54.153
on the thread here,

01:10:54.153 --> 01:10:58.813
but this does seem to have
led to something of a wake up

01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:06.640
in the curator community where
I went to the Smithsonian

01:11:06.830 --> 01:11:09.650
and they now have locks on the specimens

01:11:09.650 --> 01:11:13.830
that are referenced in
"The Feather Thief."

01:11:13.830 --> 01:11:15.160
I gave a talk in Denver

01:11:15.160 --> 01:11:19.150
and some deep pocketed
donor at the Denver Museum

01:11:20.150 --> 01:11:21.950
went up to the curators
at the end of my talk

01:11:21.950 --> 01:11:23.820
and wrote a check

01:11:23.820 --> 01:11:25.500
or committed a huge
amount of money to them

01:11:25.500 --> 01:11:27.720
to upgrade their security
for the collection

01:11:30.040 --> 01:11:33.380
which was not a desire of
mine, to be totally a honest,

01:11:33.380 --> 01:11:36.410
but a sort of unofficial
security consultant

01:11:36.410 --> 01:11:39.480
to this community where
I get emails regularly

01:11:39.480 --> 01:11:41.590
from museums around the world

01:11:41.590 --> 01:11:45.980
who are forwarding requests
for access to their collections

01:11:45.980 --> 01:11:49.770
from people who they suspect
might be salmon fly fires.

01:11:49.770 --> 01:11:51.000
And on more than one occasion,

01:11:51.000 --> 01:11:53.910
I've had to write to
these institutions to say

01:11:53.910 --> 01:11:57.730
like, no, you absolutely
should not let this person in

01:11:57.730 --> 01:11:59.280
knowing what I know about them.

01:12:01.970 --> 01:12:05.310
My wife isn't particularly
thrilled that career shift

01:12:05.310 --> 01:12:08.070
of me becoming a museum
security consultant,

01:12:08.070 --> 01:12:10.530
but even occidental out here,

01:12:10.530 --> 01:12:13.300
I live in Los Angeles and occidental,

01:12:13.300 --> 01:12:15.180
has the more lab of zoology,

01:12:15.180 --> 01:12:20.090
and I'm close friends
with the head of that lab,

01:12:20.090 --> 01:12:20.923
John McCormick.

01:12:20.923 --> 01:12:23.383
He just sent me, proudly,

01:12:25.057 --> 01:12:27.310
like a screenshot of all
of their new surveillance

01:12:27.310 --> 01:12:29.289
and security measures
and things like that.

01:12:29.289 --> 01:12:33.200
So I think there are probably new people

01:12:33.200 --> 01:12:35.330
that have entered this community fly time,

01:12:35.330 --> 01:12:38.040
but it's also, I think,
become a lot harder

01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:39.213
to steal these things.

01:12:40.730 --> 01:12:43.160
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:12:43.160 --> 01:12:46.110
Well, I'm sorry that we won't have time

01:12:46.110 --> 01:12:49.090
to get into all the rest of the questions,

01:12:49.090 --> 01:12:50.170
but at this point,

01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:53.900
I wanna thank you, the
audience for your attendance

01:12:53.900 --> 01:12:55.033
and thanks, Kirk,

01:12:56.280 --> 01:12:59.770
and best wishes, best good luck

01:12:59.770 --> 01:13:02.120
with the launch of the new book.

01:13:02.120 --> 01:13:05.220
And I look forward to
that read as well as this.

01:13:05.220 --> 01:13:08.270
And I know we'd have a
great uproarious applause

01:13:08.270 --> 01:13:09.450
if we were all together,

01:13:09.450 --> 01:13:12.100
but you certainly have mine.

01:13:12.100 --> 01:13:13.430
Thank you very much, Kirk.

01:13:13.430 --> 01:13:15.605
- Thank you. Thank you all for having me.

01:13:15.605 --> 01:13:16.870
- Thank you, Rick.

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:19.230
And thank you everyone for attending.

01:13:19.230 --> 01:13:20.063
Thank you, Kirk.

01:13:20.063 --> 01:13:21.400
And thanks once again,

01:13:21.400 --> 01:13:25.980
Ty and Guy for helping
make this such a success.

01:13:25.980 --> 01:13:28.263
Bye everyone, and see you next week.

